# Next steps for {{ workspace_name }}

This workspace IS the model. You build it incrementally through phases.

## Setup (now)

- [ ] `git init && git add -A && git commit -m "feat(stage-0): workspace bootstrap"`
- [ ] Create the venv: `uv venv .venv`
- [ ] **Activate it** in your shell: `source .venv/bin/activate`
      *(every new shell needs this; subsequent commands assume the venv is active)*
- [ ] Install workspace deps: `uv pip install -e ".[dev]"`
- [ ] Lint: `python3 scripts/lint-workspace.py` should print `workspace lint: OK`

> **Push to GitHub later.** Push once you have some structure to show.
> `gh repo create <org>/{{ workspace_name }} --public --source=. --remote=origin --push`

## Workspace inputs (load anytime)

Researchers keep loading inputs throughout a project. None of these are
sequential stages.

- **Imports** — external models you reference, fork, or operate-in-place against
- **Datasets** — experimental data the model compares against
- **References** — paper PDFs (drop a PDF; pypdf auto-extracts metadata)
- **Expert knowledge** — curated PDFs, lab notes, expert reviews

Each + Add creates a feature branch you review and PR.

### Imports (optional)

If your project builds on an existing model, register it in one of three modes:

- **reference** — read-only checkout under `external/<name>/`
- **fork-source** — catalog-only; no checkout
- **in-place** — full checkout under `external/<name>/`

```
# plugin path
/pbg-import-models <name> --source <url> --ref <ref> --mode reference

# no-plugin path
# Edit workspace.yaml.imports directly, then:
git submodule add <source> external/<name>
```

## Setup the model

Pick what the model tracks and how to render it:

- **Observables** — state variables you'll track (e.g. `chromosome.DnaA_count`)
- **Visualizations** — how to render them (time-series / phase-space / heatmap / histogram)

```
# plugin path
/pbg-add-observable
/pbg-add-visualization

# dashboard path
bash scripts/serve.sh
# use the + Add observable / + Add visualization buttons in the Setup section
```

## Build phases

Phases are the iterative model-development steps. Each phase:

- Has an objective and a Phase Gate
- Cites a source artifact (expert doc / dataset / reference)
- Adds mechanisms to the composite via processes
- Includes acceptance tests
- Lands as its own PR

```
# Generate mechanism plan (dashboard or terminal)
bash scripts/add-phase-plan.sh

# Start a mechanism phase
bash scripts/start-phase.sh <n>

# Evaluate the gate when done
bash scripts/evaluate-phase-gate.sh <n>
```

## Run

- `bash scripts/serve.sh` — local dashboard with all of the above
- `pytest -v tests/` — runs the test suite
- `python3 scripts/render-dashboard.py` — re-render the static dashboard

## Two paths

- **Browser dashboard** (`bash scripts/serve.sh`) is the primary UI for loading inputs, registering observables and visualizations, and tracking phases. No plugin needed.
- **Claude Code + the [pbg-superpowers plugin](https://github.com/vivarium-collective/pbg-superpowers)** complements the dashboard with `/pbg-phase <n>` for the iterative phase-building work that benefits from Claude's help — writing process code, integrating mechanisms from expert docs, dispatching `/pbg-expert` to wrap a missing simulator. The plugin's other skills (`/pbg-workspace`, `/pbg-server`, `/pbg-report`) automate the surrounding plumbing.

## Config files to know

- `workspace.yaml` — canonical state (observables, visualizations, phases, imports, datasets, …)
- `docs/decisions.yaml` — append-only design log
- `references/{papers.bib, claims.yaml}` — bibliography + claim → paper mapping
- `.pbg/schemas/` — workspace + phase JSON schemas (used by `lint-workspace.py`)

## Schema migration

If you have a v1 workspace (with `models[]`), run:

```
python3 scripts/_migrate_v1_to_v2.py
```

This lifts the first model's phases/observables/visualizations to the top level and bumps `schema_version` to 2.

## When stuck

1. Run `python3 scripts/lint-workspace.py` — most workspace-shape issues surface here.
2. Open the relevant `SKILL.md` in `pbg-superpowers/skills/`.
3. File an issue at <https://github.com/vivarium-collective/pbg-superpowers/issues>.
